#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097755} :activity/id #uuid "ef256051-a6fb-5783-ad98-e5e259f3af33" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050604 :topic/id #uuid "57cd3867-de9d-5ea0-b2c4-74cd8e11123f" :topic/math-academy-id 1057} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097892 :tutorial/content "For a set of $n$ paired observations $x_i$ and $y_i,$ the trend line is the straight line that best fits the data.\n\n![](images/a2/a23a1411b40f723b19446d0838346dd74dd7bf75da918859cc688171e808b44a.png)\n\nFinding the equation of the trend line is called **linear regression**. The trend line is commonly called the **regression line** or **line of best fit.**\n\nWe can show that the equation of the trend line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- The slope $m$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $y$-intercept $b$ is given by\n\n$$\nb = \\overline{y} - m \\overline{x}.\n$$\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the means of the data sets:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i, \\qquad \\overline{y} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\n$$\n\n- The quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nThere's some nice intuition behind the formula for $b.$ The trend line *always* passes through the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}).$ Therefore, this point must satisfy the trend line equation $y = mx+b.$ We can then solve this equation for $b.$\n\nAlso, notice that $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are the summation terms that feature in the formulas for the variance and covariance, respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "13ca983a-da47-572a-911a-813833c034d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5967 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77b46578-dd53-5487-a176-6e3dc8e5c0e5" :step/math-academy-i